    slug: "vacuum-forming-vs-thermoforming-enclosures",
    title: "Vacuum forming vs pressure forming for enclosures — the detail-resolution gap",
    subtitle: "Draft angles, wall thickness variation, and the tooling costs that decide",
    category: "process",
    author: "3DBuildBot Engineering",
    authorRole: "Manufacturing Engineer",
    publishedAt: "2026-07-14",
    readMinutes: 5,
    intro: "Both processes drape heated thermoplastic sheet over a mold. Pressure forming adds compressed air to push the sheet into fine details. The difference matters when the enclosure has to look injection-molded but the volume can't justify the tool.",
    body: [
      { heading: "Process mechanics", paragraph: "Vacuum forming heats a sheet of ABS, PETG, PC, or HDPE to its forming temperature (typically 150-190 C), then pulls vacuum through porous or vented tooling to draw the sheet down. Draft angles of 3-5 degrees minimum, no undercuts, feature radii of at least 2x sheet thickness. Pressure forming does everything vacuum does plus applies 40-70 psi of pressurized air on the opposite side, forcing the sheet into sharp corners, textures, and lettering. Undercuts up to about 3 mm are achievable with side actions." },
      { heading: "Tooling cost", paragraph: "Vacuum form tools can be as cheap as $800-$2,500 for a machined aluminum single-cavity tool with vent holes. Wood or MDF masters are used for prototypes at a few hundred dollars but wear fast. Pressure form tools run $4,000-$15,000 for the same part footprint due to higher tool integrity requirements and side-action mechanisms. Compared to injection molding tooling at $25,000-$80,000 for a comparable enclosure, both forming processes are cheap." },
      { heading: "Wall thickness reality", paragraph: "Sheet starts uniform, say 3 mm, and thins during forming — deep-draw areas can end up at 40-60% of starting thickness. A 200 mm deep enclosure formed from 3 mm sheet may be 1.2 mm at the corners of the deep pocket. Structural analysis has to account for this. Doubling sheet thickness helps but increases material cost proportionally and can prevent full corner definition." },
      { heading: "Surface finish and secondary operations", paragraph: "Sheet has an as-extruded finish that transfers directly to the show side. Textured sheet gives a leather-grain or matte look with no tooling texture cost. The mold side of the part is smoother and picks up any tool marks. All formed parts require trimming (5-axis routing or hand trim) which adds $2-8 per part depending on complexity. Painting, screen printing, and adhesive labeling are standard post-op for consumer product looks." },
      { heading: "Volume and application fit", paragraph: "Vacuum forming: prototypes and production up to a few thousand for kiosks, appliance housings, dunnage trays, medical carts. Pressure forming: production 500-25,000 units for enclosures needing injection-mold aesthetics — server bezels, medical device covers, professional audio equipment. Above 25,000 units, injection molding is almost always cheaper per part despite higher tooling." },
    ],
    keywords: "vacuum forming pressure forming thermoforming enclosure ABS PETG tooling",
