    title: "Oil-and-gas downhole tool material selection — Inconel 718 vs 4340",
    subtitle: "H2S, temperature, and cost tradeoffs for a tool that has to come back up",
    category: "case-study",
    author: "3DBuildBot Engineering",
    authorRole: "Materials Engineer",
    publishedAt: "2026-07-26",
    readMinutes: 6,
    intro: "A downhole logging tool operator needed to spec structural housings for a tool going into 6,500 m sour-service wells at 175 C bottom-hole temperature. The 4340 versus Inconel 718 decision cost or saved them $28K per tool depending on which way they went.",
    body: [
      { heading: "The environment", paragraph: "Downhole conditions: 175 C, 15,000 psi hydrostatic pressure, H2S partial pressure over 0.05 psi (technically 'sour' per NACE MR0175), and chloride-brine drilling fluid. The tool would rotate at up to 250 rpm during logging runs and be subjected to axial impact when pulled out of tight zones. Duty cycle: 40-50 runs per year, each 4-8 hours downhole." },
      { heading: "Why 4340 was tempting", paragraph: "AISI 4340 quenched and tempered to 30-32 HRC has excellent toughness, yields around 862 MPa, and costs about $18/kg in 6-inch bar stock. The whole tool housing set could be machined from 4340 for roughly $12K in material. Under NACE MR0175, 4340 is acceptable for sour service if hardness is under 22 HRC — but at 22 HRC the yield drops to 620 MPa and the tool wall gets uncomfortably thick for the pressure rating." },
      { heading: "Why Inconel 718 won", paragraph: "Inconel 718 in solution-treated and aged condition yields 1050 MPa at 175 C, retains toughness in H2S, and NACE MR0175 permits its use to 43 HRC. Cost: material was $95/kg, so the housing set was $52K in raw stock — a $40K premium over 4340. Machining was slow (Inconel is famously tough on tools) adding another $6K in shop time. Total premium: $28K per tool over an equivalent 4340 design at 22 HRC." },
      { heading: "The failure math", paragraph: "The customer estimated a downhole tool failure cost roughly $180K in lost logging time, fishing operations to retrieve broken hardware, and rig standby. A 4340 tool at reduced hardness was estimated to have failure rate 3-5x higher than Inconel over 5-year service. Expected failure cost differential over 5 years favored Inconel by roughly $200K, dwarfing the $28K material premium. The math was easy once the failure economics were in the analysis." },
      { heading: "Design tweaks that saved cost", paragraph: "Not every part of the tool needed Inconel. Non-pressure-containing structural elements (mounting brackets, cable clamps, internal frames) were spec'd in 316L stainless with acceptable corrosion performance. Rotating shafts subject to fatigue but not directly wetted were 17-4 PH H1150M (double-aged for stress-corrosion resistance). Only the outer pressure housing and any wetted seal surfaces were Inconel 718. Net material cost delta versus all-4340 was closer to $18K per tool, not $28K." },
    ],
    keywords: "oil gas downhole Inconel 718 4340 NACE MR0175 sour service H2S sulfide stress",
