    title: "Drone frame optimization for FAA Part 107 sub-250g and sub-25kg limits",
    subtitle: "Weight budget engineering when the regulatory limits are the design driver",
    category: "case-study",
    author: "3DBuildBot Engineering",
    authorRole: "Aerospace Engineer",
    publishedAt: "2026-07-28",
    readMinutes: 6,
    intro: "Two drone customers, two regulatory boundaries — 250 g and 25 kg. Both projects were driven by the FAA weight class rather than performance, and both used topology optimization plus SLS to hit the number.",
    body: [
      { heading: "The 249 g quadcopter", paragraph: "The sub-250 g class avoids Part 107 registration for recreational flyers, but the commercial customer wanted the class for its lighter reporting burden and lower BVLOS restrictions in their operational area. Existing prosumer drones in this class use plastic frames because carbon fiber is too heavy after adding electronics. Target: 249 g with 4K camera, 25-minute flight time, and 100 m controlled fall survival. Frame budget: 42 g." },
      { heading: "How they hit 42 g", paragraph: "Topology optimization on the frame using nTopology, constrained by motor mount loads, camera gimbal loads, and a 5G drop condition. Output geometry was a lattice-web structure that would be impossible to machine or mold economically. Printed in SLS PA12-GF (glass filled) at 42.8 g including motor mounts and camera cage integrated as one piece. Stiffness was actually higher than the prior sheet-carbon design due to the closed-cell lattice." },
      { heading: "The 24.9 kg heavy-lift", paragraph: "The upper Part 107 limit is 25 kg (55 lb) — above that, waivers and pilot certification burden increase substantially. Customer was a construction site logistics drone lifting 12 kg payloads. Airframe target: 12.5 kg empty (payload + battery + airframe = 24.9 kg). Prior design at 16.2 kg used machined aluminum and pushed the total over 25 kg with any real fuel and load." },
      { heading: "Weight reduction path", paragraph: "Structural arms went from machined 6061 tubes with milled end fittings (1.8 kg each x 6) to CFRP tubes with SLS-printed end fittings (0.8 kg each x 6) — savings 6 kg. Motor pods went from machined aluminum housings to SLS PA12 with integrated cooling geometries — savings 1.2 kg. Central hub went from a milled aluminum plate to a lattice-optimized part in printed 7075 aluminum (LPBF) — savings 0.7 kg. Total airframe: 12.4 kg. Payload capacity restored, weight class preserved." },
      { heading: "Certification considerations", paragraph: "Sub-25 kg drones under Part 107 don't require airworthiness certification but do require registration and pilot certification. For BVLOS or over-people operations (Part 107 waivers or Part 108 as it stands), FAA scrutinizes airframe design and failure modes. Documented topology optimization with FEA reports, drop testing, and vibration testing became part of the operator's waiver submission. AM-produced structural parts required additional documentation of material properties and process control — SLS PA12-GF had adequate MPD (material property data) available through the powder supplier." },
    ],
