    slug: "hidden-costs-injection-molding-tooling",
    title: "The real hidden costs of injection molding tooling",
    subtitle: "The line items that don't appear on the tooling quote but always show up in total spend",
    category: "cost",
    author: "3DBuildBot Engineering",
    authorRole: "Cost Engineer",
    publishedAt: "2026-07-29",
    readMinutes: 6,
    intro: "The quoted tooling number is rarely the total spend. Between engineering changes, texture work, T1 samples, and tool-life reserves, the fully burdened tooling cost typically lands 30-60% above the initial quote.",
    body: [
      { heading: "T0 through T3 sample cost", paragraph: "T0 samples are the first shots off the tool. Tooling quotes usually include T0 delivery but not the shipping, inspection labor, or the redlines that follow. T1 samples are after first correction — expect a $500-$3,000 tool-change charge and 2-4 weeks in the schedule. T2 and T3 samples for texture or gate optimization run similar. Budget for at least T1-T2 sample cycles beyond the tool quote — that's typically $2,000-$8,000 in additional cost and 4-8 weeks in the timeline." },
      { heading: "Texture and finish work", paragraph: "Injection mold cavity finishes are quoted per SPI grade (A-1 mirror through D-3 dry blast) or Mold-Tech texture number. If the initial quote assumes SPI B-2 and the industrial designer wants MT-11020 leather grain, that's a $2,000-$8,000 change order per cavity. Texture work happens after T0 typically, so it also adds 2-3 weeks. Have the texture spec locked before tool release, not after." },
      { heading: "Engineering changes", paragraph: "The industry rule of thumb is that half of injection tools see at least one ECN before P1 (first production). ECN cost depends on where the change happens — steel-safe changes (adding material back to a feature) are cheap ($500-$2,000) because they can be plated up or welded and remachined. Steel-remove changes are inexpensive too. Changes requiring cavity relocation or gate move are expensive ($5,000-$25,000). Design freeze discipline pre-tooling saves this." },
      { heading: "Tool life reserves and consumables", paragraph: "Aluminum tools are typically rated for 10,000-50,000 shots. Steel tools for 500,000-2,000,000. Beyond that, expect refurbishment work — repolish, replace ejector pins, rebuild wear zones on slides. Budget 15-25% of original tool cost per major refurbishment on a high-volume steel tool, at intervals of 250K-500K shots. For aluminum, treat the tool as consumable — plan a replacement at end of rated life." },
      { heading: "Storage, transportation, and insurance", paragraph: "Physical tools weigh 200 lbs to several tons. Transport between molders (if you switch suppliers) can be $2,000-$15,000 domestic, more overseas. Long-term storage runs $50-$300/month per tool. Insurance on a large tool inventory is real ($1K-$5K/year per major tool). Second-source tool availability requires an owned tool that you can physically move — verify tool ownership language in your molder contract." },
      { heading: "The realistic loaded number", paragraph: "For a moderately complex 4-cavity injection tool quoted at $60K, expect true total spend of $85K-$110K over the first 12 months including sampling, ECNs, texture work, and shipping. Include this in the business case, not just the raw quote." },
    ],
    keywords: "injection molding tooling cost hidden T0 T1 sample ECN texture SPI",
