    body: [
      { heading: "The rule stated", paragraph: "For a hardware program with $8M total R&D spend over 18 months of active engineering, budget roughly $80K per active month for prototype iteration ($1.44M total). That covers CNC and AM parts for design engineers, PCBAs for firmware team, wire harness rework, and test fixtures. This is not tooling — this is the ongoing cost of physical iteration during design." },
      { heading: "Why it lands around 1%", paragraph: "Empirically, well-run hardware programs run 8-15 significant hardware iterations per subsystem during design. A typical mid-complexity subsystem (say, a robotic gripper subsystem) requires $60-150K in prototype parts across those iterations. Program R&D spend is dominated by engineering labor at $18-30K per FTE-month. The ratio of prototype-part-spend to labor-spend on a functioning hardware program lands around 5-8% of labor cost, and labor is typically 60-75% of R&D — hence the 1% of total-R&D-per-month heuristic." },
      { heading: "The failure mode when underfunded", paragraph: "Programs that budget under 0.5% of R&D on iteration end up rationing prototype cycles. Design engineers skip physical validation of intermediate iterations, ship untested designs to first EVT build, and discover fundamental issues at prototype build 12-16 weeks later. Rework cost is 5-10x the cost of the prototype cycles they skipped. Program delays compound." },
      { heading: "The failure mode when overfunded", paragraph: "Above ~2% of R&D per month, teams tend to over-iterate cosmetics, mockups, and configurations that don't inform key design decisions. This is rare but exists at deep-pocketed corporate innovation groups. The tell is engineers who cannot articulate what a specific iteration is meant to answer. If you can't state the question a prototype is answering, don't build it." },
      { heading: "How to structure the budget", paragraph: "Give each mechanical engineer a monthly discretionary print/machine budget in the $3-8K range. Reserve larger budget for planned integration builds (EVT, DVT). Have a small pool for 'break glass in case of emergency' rush parts. Track spend monthly against active engineering headcount, not against calendar. Skipping the analysis and just spending is worse than not tracking; tracking without allocating decision authority to engineers is bureaucratic and slow." },
    ],
    keywords: "prototype budget hardware R&D iteration cost planning engineering",
    relatedPosts: ["3d-printing-bridge-production-financial", "when-to-hire-vs-outsource-manufacturing"],
