    publishedAt: "2026-08-04",
    readMinutes: 5,
    intro: "Certificates of Conformance and AS9102 First Article Inspection reports look like paperwork but represent real quality engineering hours and equipment time. Here's what actually goes into them.",
    body: [
      { heading: "What AS9102 requires", paragraph: "AS9102 Rev C FAI documentation covers three forms: Form 1 (part identification), Form 2 (materials, processes, and special processes with certifications), and Form 3 (characteristic accountability). Every dimension, note, and requirement on the drawing must be numbered as a characteristic and inspection-verified. A moderately dimensioned aerospace bracket typically has 40-90 characteristics; a complex machined housing has 200-400." },
      { heading: "Inspection hours", paragraph: "For a 60-characteristic part on a CMM (Zeiss Contura or similar), first article inspection runs 3-5 hours of program setup, 1-2 hours per part inspected (typically 3 parts to establish process), plus 2-3 hours of report compilation. Manual inspection with hand tools adds hours. Special processes (surface finish, hardness, coating thickness) each add 30-90 minutes of measurement plus documentation." },
      { heading: "Material and process certifications", paragraph: "For each material lot: mill certificate matching heat number and specification (usually free from the mill), physical location trace from stock to part (30 min of paperwork), spec verification against drawing callout (15 min). For special processes: process source approval documentation (once per approved supplier, saves reuse), lot-specific certification from the process source (variable — plating shops give it, anodizers give it, heat treaters give it). Missing a single subtier certification voids the whole FAI package." },
      { heading: "Total cost of a first FAI", paragraph: "For a fist-sized aerospace bracket with 65 characteristics, one machined material lot, and two special processes (heat treat + anodize): approximately 12-18 hours of QA time. At an aerospace shop's loaded QA rate of $140-180/hour, that's $1,680-$3,240 in QA cost alone for the FAI. First articles are often the largest single non-recurring quality cost per part number in a program." },
      { heading: "Recurring CoC cost", paragraph: "Post-FAI, per-lot CoC packages run substantially cheaper — typically 1-2 hours of QA time to compile a lot cert, material cert package, process cert package, and lot-sample inspection report. Shop rate applied: $140-360 per production lot. For a program with 40 unique part numbers and 4 production lots per year each, expect $22K-$58K annually in ongoing CoC administrative cost — real overhead that has to be quoted into the part price." },
    ],
    keywords: "AS9102 FAI first article inspection CoC certificate conformance aerospace QA",
    relatedPosts: ["aerospace-prime-itar-audit-lessons", "cad-file-hygiene-quoting"],
