    publishedAt: "2026-08-05",
    readMinutes: 6,
    intro: "State Department ITAR registration costs $2,250 per year plus a one-time application fee. The real cost is the ongoing compliance program required to actually operate under registration. Here's when the math makes sense.",
    body: [
      { heading: "The visible costs", paragraph: "DDTC registration fee: $2,250/year for Tier 1 (under $1M USML value handled). Higher tiers scale up. One-time fee to register: $2,250. Application processing takes about 60 days. That's the whole visible fee structure — the number that makes CFOs think ITAR is cheap." },
      { heading: "The overhead you actually take on", paragraph: "Written export administration compliance program (initial dev: 40-80 hours, ongoing maintenance: 4-8 hours/month). Empowered official designation and training. Foreign national employment review and controls. Physical and network access controls for USML data. Supplier flow-down clauses and audits. Employee training program (initial and recurring). Documented technology control plans per program. Realistic first-year overhead cost for a mid-sized shop: $75,000-$180,000 including consulting, legal review, and internal FTE time." },
      { heading: "When registration is worth it", paragraph: "If USML-related work is expected to exceed roughly $500K annual revenue and the customer requires supplier ITAR registration (most defense primes do), registration pays back within the first year through work you can bid on that non-registered competitors cannot. Below $500K annual USML revenue, expect registration overhead to exceed profit on the work unless the ITAR-adjacent commercial work also justifies the compliance program." },
      { heading: "When to skip and stay pure commercial", paragraph: "If defense work would represent under 10% of revenue and the compliance program would need to be built from scratch, subcontracting through an already-registered prime is often cheaper. The prime holds ITAR, you provide services as a non-USML-touching subcontractor with the prime firewalling technical data. Loses margin (the prime marks up your work) but eliminates the entire compliance overhead." },
      { heading: "Common misconceptions", paragraph: "ITAR does not equal DoD contracting. Many DoD contracts do not touch USML technical data. FAR/DFARS clauses cover DoD contracting; ITAR covers export-controlled defense articles. Some defense work is regulated under EAR (Commerce) rather than ITAR (State) — CUI and CMMC are separate compliance obligations. Get an experienced export compliance attorney to make the jurisdictional call before assuming ITAR applies. Companies over-register more often than they under-register, and both are expensive." },
    ],
    keywords: "ITAR registration DDTC USML defense compliance export control DDTC",
