    intro: "EV vertical integration was the strategy of the early 2020s. By 2026, the pattern has shifted — companies are more selective about what they insource, and the winners are the ones who kept the right things in house.",
    body: [
      { heading: "Where Tesla settled", paragraph: "Tesla continues to insource battery cell production at its Nevada, Berlin, and Austin facilities (mixed with Panasonic and CATL supply). Motor production is fully in-house. Casting operations (giga-press aluminum body structures) are in-house and expanded to include additional structural components. Semiconductors: the Dojo/HW4 stack is designed in-house but fabricated at TSMC and Samsung. Interiors, seats, glass, and lighting are largely purchased." },
      { heading: "Rivian's mixed approach", paragraph: "Rivian designed motors in-house but partnered with Bosch on production. Battery packs assembled in-house with cells from LG and Samsung. Body panels stamped in-house at Normal, IL. Casting expansion planned for R2 line. Software stack fully in-house, similar to Tesla model. Rivian's insourcing focused on structural and powertrain areas where vertical control creates competitive differentiation." },
      { heading: "Lucid's contrast", paragraph: "Lucid took an opposite tack in some areas — powertrain motors and inverters are fully in-house and represent core IP, but body-in-white was more traditionally supplied. The company's smaller volume made deep casting insourcing uneconomical. Cell packs use LG cells with in-house pack engineering." },
      { heading: "Chinese OEMs and the CATL model", paragraph: "BYD is the extreme vertical integration case — cell chemistry, cells, packs, motors, semiconductors (partial), body, glass (partial). Their scale supports vertical depth that Western OEMs cannot match economically at their volumes. CATL sits at the other end — pure cell/pack specialist with 30%+ global market share, deeply integrated with almost every non-BYD OEM including many Western brands." },
      { heading: "The 2026 pattern", paragraph: "Successful vertical integration in EVs has narrowed to: cells and packs (contentious — economies favor specialists like CATL/LG unless you're at BYD scale), motors and inverters (usually worth insourcing for control), casting and body structures (worth insourcing for cost and quality), software and semi design (essential for differentiation). Interiors, glass, lighting, HVAC, and standard mechanical hardware have largely returned to tier-1 supplier ecosystems." },
      { heading: "Implications for suppliers", paragraph: "For contract manufacturers and machine shops, EV vertical integration means specific opportunities in prototype tooling for structural castings, low-volume specialty parts for validation programs, and tier-2 supply into the tier-1s that ultimately fabricate for OEMs. Chasing direct OEM business is possible but the qualification cycles are long and the volumes are unforgiving." },
    ],
