    intro: "Quoting speed depends heavily on file quality. Well-prepared CAD packages get quoted in hours; sloppy packages get requoted three times or bounce back for clarification.",
    body: [
      { heading: "File format essentials", paragraph: "For most CNC and AM: STEP (AP242 or AP214) is the universal format that all shops can consume. Include a native CAD file (SolidWorks, NX, Fusion, Onshape) if the shop uses your CAD system — some CAM programmers prefer native to STEP. Include a 2D drawing PDF with dimensions, tolerances, notes, and material call-out. STL is fine only for AM prototypes without tight tolerance — never send STL for CNC work." },
      { heading: "The drawing package", paragraph: "Even for AM parts, a 2D drawing with critical dimensions, tolerance notes, finish requirements, and material spec drastically reduces quoting time. Overall envelope dimensions, key mating features, hole sizes, and thread callouts should be explicit. Non-critical features can be tolerance-driven by process default (typical CNC ±0.005 in). Marking a couple of critical dimensions with tight tolerance is fine; marking every dimension with ±0.001 in is expensive and usually unnecessary." },
      { heading: "Common file problems", paragraph: "Non-manifold geometry (holes in surfaces, disconnected edges) — kills quoting. Very small features that appear as gaps at coarse tessellation. Assembly files with hundreds of components when the manufacturable geometry is one part — send individual part files. Missing material call-out — shops guess and quote conservatively (higher). Ambiguous unit systems (metric geometry with imperial drawing dimensions, or vice versa) — always causes at least one round-trip clarification." },
      { heading: "Threads, callouts, and standards", paragraph: "Threaded holes: spec by standard (M6x1.0, 1/4-20 UNC-2B) with depth or 'through' indicated. Don't model the thread geometry in CAD — shops tap or thread-mill regardless of whether the thread is modeled. Countersinks: spec by angle and major diameter or use standard callout (82° x 0.375 for #10 flathead). Surface finish: use standard notation (Ra 63 microinch or Ra 1.6 micron) with the surface area indicated." },
      { heading: "Assembly handoffs", paragraph: "When sending an assembly for quoting, provide: assembly STEP for context, individual part STEPs, individual part drawings, a BOM listing part numbers and quantities, and specific notes on which parts are supplier-fabricated versus purchased. Save the shop from having to reverse-engineer your BOM structure. A clean assembly package with 20 parts should quote in 2-4 hours; a messy one takes 2-3 days of back-and-forth." },
    ],
