    intro: "Hardware teams adopting agile-style sprint cadences run into a fundamental mismatch — software cycles are days, hardware cycles are weeks. A meeting rhythm that respects both keeps teams aligned without over-meeting.",
    body: [
      { heading: "The core problem", paragraph: "Software teams sprint on 1-2 week cadences with daily standups and work that can be completed and reviewed within a sprint. Hardware teams have design cycles that span 2-6 weeks (design, review, procurement, fabrication, delivery, test), which cannot be forced into 2-week completion boxes. Simply adopting software agile rituals to a hardware team creates fake progress reports and demoralized engineers." },
      { heading: "A rhythm that works", paragraph: "Weekly all-hands 30-min status: what's in the shop, what's arriving this week, what's blocked. Biweekly sprint boundaries with sprint-review-plus-planning of 90 min combined. Monthly deep-dive design review of 90-120 min on one specific subsystem, rotating focus. Quarterly program review of 2-3 hours with milestone assessment and re-planning if needed. Ad-hoc technical discussions as needed — don't schedule everything." },
      { heading: "Standups: yes but scoped", paragraph: "Daily standups work if scoped to sub-teams (mechanical, electrical, firmware, quality) rather than the whole hardware org. 10-15 min max, focus on blockers and cross-team handoffs. Skip if there are no blockers. Whole-hardware-team daily standup at 15+ people is meeting theater — nobody has updates worth 15 people's time daily." },
      { heading: "Sprint boundary rituals", paragraph: "Every 2 weeks: review what shipped from the shop (physical builds and test results), plan what fabrication starts this sprint (long-lead items get committed), review technical debt (parts still in machining, tests still pending). Do not force every hardware task into a sprint-completion box. Some tasks span 3 sprints — track them at higher abstraction (milestones) rather than as sprint stories." },
      { heading: "Design reviews", paragraph: "Monthly deep-dive on one subsystem — mechanical, electrical, firmware, thermal, etc. Prepared presentation from the subsystem owner, structured critique, action items tracked to closure. Better than 'design reviews' that are actually design decisions being made in real time. Real design reviews have artifacts: drawings marked up, analyses to look at, physical parts to touch. If there's nothing to review, cancel the meeting." },
      { heading: "Executive and customer updates", paragraph: "Bi-weekly or monthly executive update on program status with clear KPIs (schedule, budget, risk). Do not conflate this with team-internal meetings — the audience, prep effort, and focus are different. Bad hardware program management is one weekly meeting trying to serve internal coordination and executive reporting simultaneously. Separate them." },
    ],
    keywords: "hardware team meetings agile scrum sprint cadence engineering management",
